WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.836 --> 00:00:17.835
Oliver walked along the main road until he spotted a footpath crossing a field.

2
00:00:17.836 --> 00:00:22.914
"Mr. Bumble brought me on that path when I came to the workhouse," he said to himself.

3
00:00:22.915 --> 00:00:27.668
To avoid being noticed, he decided to leave the main road and take the path.

4
00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:33.144
He soon reached Mrs. Mann's farm, where he had lived for the first ten years of his life.

5
00:00:33.145 --> 00:00:39.315
No one was stirring at this early hour. Oliver passed by silently and kept on walking.

6
00:00:39.316 --> 00:00:43.283
When the path ended, he found himself back on the main road.

7
00:00:43.284 --> 00:00:51.712
The sun was shining brightly now, and Oliver was still afraid someone might see him. So he alternately ran and hid behind bushes.

8
00:00:51.713 --> 00:00:59.053
At noon he sat down by a milestone to think about where to go. The milestone said it was seventy miles to London.

9
00:00:59.054 --> 00:01:07.464
"London!" thought Oliver. "Nobody could ever find me in a huge city. Not even Mr. Bumble."

10
00:01:07.465 --> 00:01:13.194
He had often heard the old men in the workhouse say that an enterprising boy would never starve in London.

11
00:01:13.195 --> 00:01:20.956
There were ways to earn a living in the city that country people couldn't even imagine. Oliver jumped to his feet and started walking again.

12
00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:29.289
He walked for five more miles before he realized how long it would take him to get to London. Sitting down to rest, he opened his bundle.

13
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:42.421
"A clean shirt is a very comfortable thing," thought Oliver. "And so are two pairs of darned stockings. A penny and a crust of bread are good too, but they're of little help on a 65-mile walk in wintertime."

14
00:01:42.422 --> 00:01:51.929
Oliver walked twenty miles that day. He ate only the crust of dry bread and drank only a few drinks of water begged from cottages along the road.

15
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.913
That night he went into a meadow and slept under a hay wagon.

16
00:01:55.914 --> 00:02:00.913
At first he was frightened by the wind moaning dismally over the empty fields.

17
00:02:00.914 --> 00:02:06.230
He had never felt so lonely, but exhausted from his journey, he soon fell asleep.

18
00:02:06.231 --> 00:02:11.492
The next morning he was so hungry that he had to use his penny to buy a small loaf of bread.

19
00:02:11.493 --> 00:02:15.468
He had walked no more than 12 miles when night closed in again.

20
00:02:15.469 --> 00:02:21.242
His feet were sore and his legs were weak. He spent another night outdoors.

21
00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:24.128
The days passed and Oliver kept walking.

22
00:02:24.128 --> 00:02:30.785
In some villages he saw large signs warning that beggars would be sent to jail, so he hurried through those villages.

23
00:02:30.786 --> 00:02:35.793
In other places he hung around the inns, hoping someone would give him something to eat.

24
00:02:35.794 --> 00:02:40.872
He begged at farmhouses, but usually the farmers threatened to set the dogs on him.

25
00:02:40.873 --> 00:02:47.380
Oliver would have starved to death without the bread and cheese given to him by a kindhearted toll collector and an old lady.

26
00:02:47.381 --> 00:02:52.451
On the seventh morning after he had run away, he slowly limped into a little town.

27
00:02:52.452 --> 00:02:58.277
The sun was rising as Oliver, with bleeding feet and dust-covered clothes, sat on a doorstep.

28
00:02:58.278 --> 00:03:04.762
A boy passed by. He returned a few minutes later and stood across the road, staring at Oliver.

29
00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:08.945
Noticing that Oliver was looking at him, the boy crossed the road.

30
00:03:08.946 --> 00:03:13.524
"Hello, my friend," said the boy. "What brings you here?"

31
00:03:13.525 --> 00:03:20.191
Oliver couldn't answer for a moment because he was busy staring at the boy, who was the strangest person he'd ever seen.

32
00:03:20.192 --> 00:03:24.262
He was definitely a boy, but he swaggered like a man.

33
00:03:24.263 --> 00:03:30.675
A hat perched precariously on top of his head, and he wore a man's coat that nearly reached his heels.

34
00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:34.845
The sleeves were so long that he had to roll them up to see his hands.

35
00:03:34.846 --> 00:03:42.488
"I'm hungry and tired," Oliver finally replied with tears in his eyes. "I've been walking for seven days."

36
00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:50.321
"Seven days!" exclaimed the boy. "That's a long time. If you want food, you shall have it!"

37
00:03:50.322 --> 00:03:57.639
Helping Oliver to stand up, the boy took him to a nearby shop. There he purchased a ham and a loaf of bread.

38
00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.470
Then he led Oliver to a pub, where he bought something to drink and urged Oliver to eat the bread and ham.

39
00:04:03.471 --> 00:04:07.653
"Going to London?" asked the strange boy when Oliver had finished eating.

40
00:04:07.654 --> 00:04:10.065
"Yes," answered Oliver.

41
00:04:10.066 --> 00:04:12.629
"Got any lodgings?"

42
00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.874
"No."

43
00:04:13.898 --> 00:04:15.439
"Got any money?"

44
00:04:15.463 --> 00:04:16.739
"No."

45
00:04:16.764 --> 00:04:19.114
The boy whistled and shook his head.

46
00:04:19.114 --> 00:04:22.034
"Do you live in London?" asked Oliver.

47
00:04:22.035 --> 00:04:26.796
"Yes, I do. I suppose you'll be wanting someplace to sleep tonight."

48
00:04:26.797 --> 00:04:32.613
"Oh, yes," replied Oliver. "I haven't slept under a roof since I left the country."

49
00:04:32.614 --> 00:04:41.010
"Well, don't fret about that. I have to be in London tonight. I know a respectable old gentleman who'll give you lodgings for free."

50
00:04:41.011 --> 00:04:45.208
This unexpected offer was too tempting for Oliver to pass up.

51
00:04:45.209 --> 00:04:50.192
He and the boy continued to talk, and Oliver discovered that his name was Jack Dawkins.

52
00:04:50.193 --> 00:04:53.923
"But my friends call me the Artful Dodger," said the boy.

53
00:04:53.924 --> 00:04:58.862
For some reason the Dodger didn't want to arrive in London before nightfall.

54
00:04:58.863 --> 00:05:02.600
So it was nearly eleven o'clock when they entered the great city.

55
00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:08.259
Oliver followed closely behind as his new friend led him into one of the worst parts of the city.

56
00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:16.164
Oliver had never seen a dirtier or more wretched place. The streets were narrow and muddy, and the air smelled of filth.

57
00:05:16.165 --> 00:05:21.084
At last the Dodger pushed open the door to an old house and whistled loudly.

58
00:05:21.085 --> 00:05:24.842
"Now then!" a strange voice said in reply.

59
00:05:24.843 --> 00:05:30.826
The Dodger uttered a mysterious password and then led Oliver upstairs to a dark and dirty room.

60
00:05:30.827 --> 00:05:35.826
Four boys sat at a table watching an old man cook sausages over the fire.

61
00:05:35.827 --> 00:05:39.580
The boys gathered around as the Dodger whispered to the man.

62
00:05:39.581 --> 00:05:46.803
Suddenly the Dodger said, "Fagin, this is my friend Oliver Twist," and everyone turned to look.

63
00:05:46.804 --> 00:05:52.054
Now Oliver could see that the man's matted red hair covered much of an ugly face.

64
00:05:52.055 --> 00:05:56.792
"Pleased to meet you," he said, taking Oliver's hand and bowing low.

65
00:05:56.793 --> 00:06:02.038
The boys crowded around Oliver, shaking his hands and putting their hands into his pockets.

66
00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:07.117
Fagin watched as Oliver stared at the many silk handkerchiefs hanging about the room.

67
00:06:07.118 --> 00:06:13.696
"I see you've noticed our handkerchiefs," said Fagin. "We're just getting them ready for the wash."

68
00:06:13.697 --> 00:06:21.692
The boys all burst into laughter. Oliver was puzzled, but he didn't say anything because the sausages were done and it was time to eat.

69
00:06:21.693 --> 00:06:26.874
After supper Oliver settled down on an old sack and went right to sleep on the floor.

70
00:06:26.875 --> 00:06:32.478
He could wait until tomorrow to begin his new life in this strange place.
